<%
  const _lang = page.lang || 'en';
  function findPage(permalink) {
    let match = null;
    site.pages.forEach(p => {
      const pp = p.canonical_path || p.permalink;
      if (pp !== permalink) return;
      if (p.lang === _lang && p.content) { match = p; }
      else if (!match && p.content) { match = p; }
    });
    return match;
  }
  const aboutPage = findPage('/about/');
  const researchPage = findPage('/research/');
%>

<canvas class="lattice-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

<section class="hero" id="home" data-section="0" data-morph="0">
  <div class="hero-content container">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">Rock & Code · Field Notes</p>
    <h1 class="hero-tagline">Exploring geology through code</h1>
    <p class="hero-subtagline">Incoming Master's · Geological Engineering · Heilongjiang University of Science &amp; Technology</p>
    <a href="#about" class="hero-cta" data-smooth>
      Get to know me <span class="arrow">↓</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story-section" id="about" data-section="1" data-morph="1">
  <div class="story-panel">
    <div class="section-head"><h2 class="section-title">About</h2></div>
    <div class="story-content">
      <p>Hi, I'm <strong>Liang Chengyi</strong>, from Liangping, Chongqing. I'm about to begin my Master's in <strong>Geological Engineering</strong> at <strong>Heilongjiang University of Science &amp; Technology</strong>.</p>
      <p>My undergraduate background is in computer science. After graduating I spent some time writing code in industry — I built a complete Java backend system and maintained business systems like a CRM. Honestly, coming into geology wasn't something I'd planned from the start. But the more I looked at it, the more it made sense for me — geological engineering research increasingly relies on computation and data, and a lot of geology-background groups might be looking for exactly this: someone who can help with data, build small tools, and write decent code. If an advisor happens to have that kind of need, I think I can be of use.</p>
      <h3>On my geology foundation</h3>
      <p>This is a gap I know I have to close, and I won't pretend otherwise. My plan is to work through the fundamentals systematically, and ask and look things up when I don't understand. The good news is I pick up new things quickly, and I can sit with a subject.</p>
      <h3>Where I can lend a hand</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>If the group has work involving data, code, or tools, I can get going on it — without being taught entirely from scratch. The assessment system was a big project, and I took it from requirements all the way to deployment.</li>
        <li>When I run into something I haven't done, I'll figure it out myself first — look it up, try it — rather than just waiting. This blog, theme and deployment included, is something I built that way.</li>
        <li>Tedious or iterative work is fine with me too — two months of daily patching on business systems taught me to see things through.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

