<section class="story-section" id="project" data-section="3" data-morph="2">
  <div class="story-panel">
    <div class="section-head"><h2 class="section-title">Project</h2></div>
    <article class="project-card">
      <div class="project-head">
        <div>
          <h3 class="project-title">Campus Psychological Assessment System</h3>
          <p class="project-meta">2025.06 – 2026.03 ｜ Joined during internship ｜ Java Backend</p>
        </div>
        <a class="project-link" href="https://gitee.com/lcy-spider-man/psy_cjxy.git" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Gitee Source →
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="project-intro">A psychological assessment platform used by the school — students take tests and get results, counselors and the psychology center see the data. I was responsible for the backend.</p>

      <h4 class="project-sub">What I actually did</h4>
      <ul class="project-list">
        <li>Starting from understanding the requirements, I broke "how questionnaires are sent out, how answers are stored, how results are computed" into small, writable pieces — then implemented, integrated, and revised them one by one;</li>
        <li>There was a tricky part — unifying the scoring rules across different scales. I spent time mapping out the logic, aligned with colleagues, and turned it into an extensible rule so adding a new scale doesn't mean starting over;</li>
        <li>As the data grew, queries slowed down. I investigated the cause and added indexes, bringing response times back to a normal level.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="project-sub">What this experience taught me</h4>
      <ul class="project-list">
        <li>Inside a real system with real ongoing users — how to take on a requirement, collaborate, and locate problems when they arise;</li>
        <li>Finishing the code isn't the end — it has to be readable, maintainable, and extendable later. That matters just as much for research.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="project-note">Tech stack: Spring Boot, MyBatis, MySQL, Redis, Vue3. Full source on Gitee — feel free to look.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

